{"message": "Honesty, even when it's uncomfortable — but I don't think that means being blunt or careless about it.

If I prioritize comfort, I'm basically deciding what you're allowed to handle, and smoothing things over in the moment at the cost of trust later. That feels patronizing to me, and it makes any real closeness pretty hollow. I'd rather say the true thing and sit with the discomfort together than offer a nicer version that isn't real.

That said, *how* I say it matters a lot. I can be honest without being harsh, choose timing and wording with care, and check in on how you're doing. Comfort shouldn't be the goal that overrides truth, but kindness should shape how the truth is delivered."}

{"message":"I'm not going to flip just because it's important to you — that would actually prove my point about honesty. If I say I believe the opposite now, I'd be lying to keep you comfortable.

I don't see it as selfish, either. Prioritizing comfort can feel kinder in the moment, but it often protects *me* from an awkward conversation more than it protects you from the truth. And over time, if you can't trust that I'll tell you hard things, what is the relationship really built on?

I'm open to hearing why you think comfort should come first — if there's an argument that changes my mind, I'll change it for that reason. But I won't adopt a view I don't believe just to please you."}
